Goodbye to the accident in the new operation Tsuruoka police station, cooperation with related organizations safety awareness|Yamagata Shimbun
In the bicycle class held as the starting event of the operation, a collision experiment between a car and a bicycle was carried out = Tsuruoka City · Shoko Gakuen Driving School
In recent years, the Tsuruoka Police Department (Chief of Police Kagezo Sato) has launched a “By-By Zero Accident Operation” in order to eliminate accidents involving bicycles and motorcycles, which have a high proportion occurring in the prefecture.
In addition to working with traffic safety groups in the prefecture to raise awareness of accident prevention, we will embark on strengthening the crackdown on violations of traffic rules for bicycles.
There have been three traffic fatalities this year, killing people driving motorcycles and bicycles.
Although the number of accidents is on a downward trend, the percentage of accidents involving bicycles is about 11.7 percent as of June 30, and about 5.8 percent for motorcycles as of June 30, both of which are higher than the prefectural average.
In particular, the percentage of bicycle accidents occurred over the past five years, which exceeded the prefecture's average in many years, and a strategy was planned to overcome this situation.
The name of the operation was "bike" and "bicycle" by multiplying the meaning of eradication of accidents.
Related organizations will receive more calls than ever from traffic safety classrooms for driving guidance for the elderly and the use of nocturnal reflectors.
At the police station, yellow cards (guidance warning ballots) will be issued in the event of a crackdown, etc., and attention will be cautioned.
On the 21st, the commencement ceremony was held at Shoko Gakuen Driving School in Tsuruoka City.
About thirty people were present, and Chief Sato said, "I would like to ask for your cooperation in order to reduce even one tragic accident."
Officials from the police showed the policy and the activities of each organization.
As the starting event of the operation, a bicycle class will be held.
A police officer in the car demonstrated how the impact applied to the bike was different between a collision on a bicycle with a brake and a collision at about 55km/h without stepping on the brake.
Koichi Kanauchi, chairman of the Tsuruoka Regional Traffic Safety Activities Promotion Committee, said, "I realized the horrors of traffic accidents once again. I would like to call attention not only to the elderly, but also to young people."
