Four products of ingredients produced in the prefecture will be released sequentially from today Seven-Eleven in Mie (Ise newspaper) - Yahoo! News
In order to support businesses affected by the spread of the new coronavirus infection, convenience store giant Seven-Eleven Japan (Tokyo) will release four new products such as ramen and onigiri using ingredients from the prefecture to commemorate the 15th anniversary of the store's opening in Mie Prefecture.
The company opened the Kuwana Eba store in Kuwana City as the first store in the prefecture.
It has now expanded to 168 stores and is celebrating its 15th anniversary this year.
Commemorative products are sold using ingredients from the prefecture where demand has declined due to refraining from going out.
In addition to the prefecture, it is also available at stores in Aichi and Gifu prefectures.
On the 23rd, it will be released with cold salt ramen (440yen) made with Madai dashi stock and Taimeshi rice balls (140yen).
On the 28th, two onigiri (rice balls) (175yen) made with red shiso and raw tsukudani, as well as Ise tea cream puff (140yen) will be released.
On the 22nd, Kazutake Iinuma, deputy general manager of the company's operations headquarters, met with Governor Hidetaka Suzuki at the prefectural office and said, "The producers provided us with very wonderful ingredients and created products with sincerity.
The world is in a difficult situation, but I want to liven up Mie Prefecture through our products.”
Governor Suzuki said, "While it is an attractive product, there are only items affected by this corona, and I think it is an initiative that gives courage for producers."
I tasted ramen and Taimeshi's musubi, and said, "The soup stock in Thailand works well. It's delicious."
