Students at Okayama University's Special Needs School (Hirai, Naka-ku, Okayama City) make Bizen-yaki figurines of the yokai "Amabie", which is said to quell the plague, in order to give gifts to elderly people in the area.
Due to the influence of the new coronavirus, it is difficult to conduct sales training at stores and interact with local residents every year, so we planned it with the hope of an early settlement of infectious diseases and health.
Eleven members of the high school produced as part of their classes three days a week from June in the morning.
After molding the ground clay with a die cut, the straw is wrapped in order to bring out the red pattern unique to Bizen ware.
It is said to be completed in about 2-3 weeks, including the drying and firing processes.
A third-year student (18) said, "I'm working hard with the hope that the elderly people will not lose to the new corona.
On the 27th, Bizen ware is scheduled to be delivered to residents of Okayama City Tomorakuen (the same place), a nursing home where there is a choral performance.
Production is scheduled to continue until the end of July, and the destination of the remaining works will be decided in the future.
