"Cold water shower" brings benefits to the body and mind (Cosmopolitan) - Yahoo! News
Reduce inflammation such as muscle pain
A study published in The North American Journal of Medical Sciences examining the effectiveness of hydrotherapy (a treatment with water) found that it was expected to have an effect on physical and mental healing.
By taking cold water after exercise, muscle pain and other inflammatory effects can be suppressed.
“It’s important to exercise, take a cold shower without spending as much time as possible. After moving your body, you can immediately lower your body temperature, which will speed up recovery from inflammation and fatigue caused by exercise,” said Professor Douglas Kasa, a professor at Connecticut University who teaches sports medicine.
Reducing fatigue
If you exercise in high temperatures, your heart, muscles, and skin will need more blood flow than usual.
“When you exercise, your heart rate increases, you have a lot of blood flow to your heart, you need more energy to shrink your muscles, so you have more muscle blood. Also, when it comes to the skin, a lot of blood flows to regulate your body temperature,” Kasa says.
“By taking a cold shower, the temperature of the skin is reduced, so you can reduce the amount of blood flow sent to the skin. Instead, blood flow is sent to the internal organs such as the stomach and intestines, making it easier to take water and nutrients even after exercise. As a result, you can reduce the fatigue you feel after exercise.”
Looking forward to the hormesis effect.
Drozewsky also explains that you can expect to activate the "hormesis effect" by bathing in cold water.
Hormesis effect is the idea that substances and stress that are harmful to the body should have a little more than nothing.
“Low-oxygen, petit fasting, and cold showers are some examples of what you can expect to have a hormesis effect on your health. By taking a cold shower, hormones and neurotransmitters can be stimulated to improve physical and physical resilience and resilience.”
