Macau casino supervising authority observes the implementation status of epidemic prevention measures at casino facilities ... Presentation of a negative certificate of the new coronavirus at the time of admission (Macau newspaper) - Yahoo! News
About forty casino facilities in Macau were closed for 15 days from February 5th to 19th during the COVID-19 pandemic, and have resumed operations after taking strict epidemic prevention measures after the 20th of the same month.
The Macau Casino Regulatory Agency (DICJ) requires the operating company to protect the health of its employees and guests as much as possible, ensuring the distance between gaming (casino) tables, allowing three to four people to sit at the same time in a baccarat table (e.g., three to four people who can sit at one table at the same time in a baccarate table), and reducing the risk of cross-infection by reducing the risk of cross-infection.
Disinfection of gaming products such as chips has also been strengthened.
In addition, guests are required to wear a mask at the time of admission, take a temperature, and present an effective health code (the most recent history of stay, the presence or absence of contact with new coronavirus patients, the presence or absence of symptoms such as fever and cough, and those that are generated by entering contact information).
On July 15th, from midnight on July 15th, a negative certificate of new coronavirus nucleic acid test (limited to those within the validity period issued by the authorized facility in Macau or Guangdong Province) has also been added as a condition.
From 6am on the 15th, there is a relaxation of border control measures between Macau and Guangdong Province (elimination of quarantine for fourteen days, with various conditions), and it will be a precautionary measure associated with this.
In response to the change in entry conditions from the 15th of the same month on July 18th, the DICJ announced that in response to the change in the entry conditions from the 15th of the same month, the bureau's Director-General, Adriano Ho, and others observed the implementation of epidemic prevention measures at the casino facility, held a meeting where representatives of the security departments of the six casino operators were convened, and by thoroughly managing the entry of guests, it was asked again to protect the health of employees and guests.
DICJ said it will continue to carry out strict patrol patrols on casino facilities and have regular meetings with operating companies according to the situation and evaluation, and will adjust epidemic prevention measures at casinos as appropriate in accordance with the guidelines of the government health bureau.
The visit to this casino facility was also accompanied by Mr. Hayashimatsu of the Centers for Disease Supervision and Prevention of the Hygiene Bureau, and gave guidance on disinfection of gaming (casino) chips at the site and how to take a safe distance between the seats of electronic casino equipment.
Due to a sharp decrease in inbound tourists due to epidemic prevention measures, including entry restrictions, casino sales for the January-June quarter decreased by 77.4% from the same period of the previous year to 33.2 billion Macau Pataca (Japanese yen equivalent: about 4519 billion yen).
