Masujiro Omura's Episody as an Educator|Ube Nippo Komachi Media Web
On the 23rd, Masujiro Omura (1825-69), one of the ten outstanding figures of the Meiji Restoration, appeared at his birthplace of Gensenji, Yamaguchi City.
There was an unveiling ceremony in the front garden of the Gensenji Regional Exchange Center, which was established, and local and government officials celebrated the completion.
Masujiro was a Western scholar and doctor at the end of the Edo period during the Meiji Restoration.
After teaching at the Bunsho Shirabesho, a research and educational institution for Western studies by the shogunate, he opened a private school in Edo called "Hatoido" and taught Western studies, military studies, and medicine.
Even after returning to the Choshu Domain, he gave a lecture on Hyogaku at Meirin-kan, Yamaguchi.
The statue focused on Masujiro as an educator.
Standing next to the globe and holding a book in his hand, he gave a lecture on Western studies.
The height of the pedestal is about three meters (one and fifty-five meters), and it is said that it was made from the eyes of a disciple who was lectured by Masujiro.
It was designed by Masujiro's wife, Kotoko's descendant, and local designer Marie Takami, 27.
It was erected by the 150th anniversary project executive committee (chaired by Satoshi Okamoto) after Masujiro OMURA's death, which is based of the Seisenji Local Government Association, solicited donations and built it.
About 128million yen were collected from 1104 people from all over the country.
The cost of the project, including the installation of the famous board, is about twelve million yen.
The remaining donations will be used for the maintenance and maintenance of the statue.
Mr. Okamoto said, "Masujiro, who has continued to have high aspirations and made a great contribution as an educator, is proud of Furusato, and it is a statue that serves as the hope of the children of the region who will be responsible for the future."
