Masuda-jinja Shrine cancels the annual grand festival Martyr's sergeant's festivary to make efforts to prevent cholera The effects of the coronavirus affecting only Shinto rituals today|News from the town|Saga Shimbun News|Saga Shimbun LiVE
Masuda-jinja Shrine enshrines Officer Masuda who was martyred in order to prevent cholera = Tano, Hizen-cho, Karatsu City
In July, Officer Masuda rode on a white horse paraded through the district = Tano, Hizen-cho, Karatsu City (provided)
Masuda-jinja Shrine, located in Takagushi district of Tano, Hizen-cho, Karatsu City, is dedicated to combating cholera, an infectious disease that spread to the area 125 years ago, and is dedicated to the martyred officer Keitaro Masuda (1869-95).
Every year, an annual festival has been held in late July, but this year it was canceled due to the influence of the new coronavirus.
On the 26th, the self-governing president and others will perform only Shinto rituals.
The annual festival was held by a self-governing association, such as a parade of Mr. Masuda's floats and the launch of fireworks.
Nearly two-hundred people, including bereaved families living in Kumamoto Prefecture, police officials, and local residents, attend every year.
This year, the number of interviews from the media has increased due to the coronavirus pandemic, and Yoshinori Takeda, 72, the chairman of the local government, says, "It's crowded every year at an annual festival that has been connected to the local community, and it's very disappointing."
The prefectural police also canceled a memorial service visited by police academy students this year.
On the 21st, Yumiko Sugiuchi, the head of the department, and Hirohito Fukushima, the head of the Karatsu police station, visited Komatsu Island off the coast of Takagushi Port, which was attached to Dabi, and offered flowers to the monument.
Mr. Sugiuchi renewed his thought, “The brave and dedicated spirit is the starting point of the police, and I want to pass it on as the ideal form.”
Officer Masuda, who was born in Kumamoto Prefecture, was assigned to Takagushi in July 1895.
He took measures such as disinfecting the patient's house without sleep, and later he himself was infected with cholera.
"I'm going to carry all the cholera of Takakushi on my back," he said, and he died at the age of twenty-five on the 4th day.
